  "## The instruction

  『b + amu native binary で llama.cpp, vllm, sglang なみの成熟度を実現するために
  必要なことを全て行う』 — option (b) was *qualify f64 for the native word-typed
  walk*, and then everything needed for maturity.

  Two answers, and the first one changes the second.

  ## (b) turned out to be already half-true, and the other half landed

  The rule said `only-native-word-typed-features?` rejects f64 expressions
  (`kernel_math_core.kotoba`'s own docstring). Measured: **false**.
  `native-f64-arithmetic-operations` has admitted `f64-add/sub/mul/div/min/max/
  abs/neg/sqrt` and the bit conversions since ADR-2608030300, and

      (defn add2 [a :i64 b :i64] :i64
        (f64-to-bits (f64-add (f64-from-bits a) (f64-from-bits b))))

  compiled to aarch64 before anything changed. What was refused was the
  **signature**: `(defn add2 [x :f64 y :f64] :f64 (f64-add x y))` answered
  *typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target …*. Float values
  were always allowed through the machine; only saying so was not. That is why
  every float kernel here is written in the bit-word idiom, `kotoba-lang/
  aiueos`'s qwen35 kernels included.

  So (b) was: admit `:f64` at the native function boundary. Landed in both
  halves, because either alone is a wrong answer — `kotoba.kir` alone is a
  green `amu check` and a red `amu compile`:

  | repo | commit |
  |---|---|
  | `kotoba-lang/osaho` (`kotoba.kir`) | `74426bad` |
  | `kotoba-lang/kotoba-verifier` | `39db2f3f` |
  | `kotoba-lang/amu` (pin advance) | PR #905 |

  Verified through `bin/amu` after the pin advance, not through a patched
  classpath — `(defn scale [x :f64] :f64 (f64-mul x 2.5))` extracts to

      d2e80081   MOVZ X1, #0x4004, LSL #48     ; 2.5
      9e670000   FMOV D0, X0
      9e670021   FMOV D1, X1
      1e610800   FMUL D0, D0, D1
      9e660002   FMOV X2, D0
      d65f03c0   RET

  A record FIELD stays refused, and both repositories keep a test for that:
  `native-word-field-types`'s own reason — a field \"would have to say which
  width its one word carries, and no field's declared type is read at runtime\"
  — does not reach a signature, where the width IS the declaration. `:f32` is
  not admitted: both widths are one word and the argument covers both, but only
  `:f64` was measured end to end, and an admission is a claim about what a
  backend lowers rather than about what an argument covers.

  ## What (b) does NOT unlock, and this is the whole roadmap

  A dot product needs more than one value. On a general native target there is
  no way to give it one:

  - `:vector-f64` is a **private handle**: `native-private-handle-type?`
    admits it at an INTERNAL call boundary and `native-function-boundary-type?`
    refuses it for an export. \"Parameters still cannot be handles.\"
  - bytes do not exist: amu's `bytes-operations` table is exactly
    `{bytes-empty 0}` — a constructor for the empty one and nothing else.
  - `kotoba-lang/aiueos` HAS the byte half in Kotoba, and it is not a sketch:
    `qwen35-gguf-header-valid`, `-kv-scan`, `-tensor-table-bind`,
    `-vocab-index-build`, `-dequant-row`, `-dot-f32`, `-matvec`, `-norm`,
    `-activation`, `-attention`, `-recurrent-step`, `-tokenize`, `-detokenize`,
    each compiled to a `.o` with provenance against the `kernel/qwen35_infer.c`
    oracle. They compile for `x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1` only: elsewhere amu
    answers *bounded kernel memory operation requires the aiueos kernel
    target*.

  **The primitive exists. It is unreachable from a general host.** That single
  sentence is the difference between an arithmetic kernel and an inference
  engine, and every item below is ordered behind it.

  ## What llama.cpp / vLLM / SGLang maturity actually consists of

  Stated plainly because the instruction was `全て`: this is not a sprint.
  llama.cpp is years of kernel work by a large contributor base; vLLM and
  SGLang are serving layers over PyTorch/CUDA whose value is scheduling, not
  arithmetic. What follows is the ordered list of what would have to exist,
  with what this workspace already has against each.

  | # | capability | what exists here today |
  |---|---|---|
  | P0 | **general-target memory** | aiueos `kernel-*` ops, kernel target only |
  | P1 | quantized matvec (Q4_K/Q6_K/Q8_0) | **emitted on x86-64** as `kernel-dequant-dot-{q8-0,q4-k,q6-k}`; **absent on aarch64**; IQ formats declared and refused. See the amendment |
  | P2 | attention, RoPE, RMSNorm, SwiGLU | `qwen35-attention`, `-norm`, `-activation`, `-recurrent-step`; `kernel_math_core` for the pure half |
  | P3 | KV cache, paged attention | **decisions landed** in Kotoba (`scheduler_core.kotoba`); block table itself blocked on P0 |
  | P4 | continuous batching, chunked prefill | **decisions landed**; the sequence list is the host's |
  | P5 | prefix/radix caching, speculative decoding, MTP | **decisions landed**; the radix trie is blocked on P0. `kotodama.inference.mtp` contract |
  | P6 | sampling, grammar-constrained decoding | `kotodama.inference.sample` |
  | P7 | GGUF/safetensors loading, mmap | `kotodama.inference.gguf` (`.cljc`, host-side) |
  | P8 | SIMD / Metal / CUDA | `num` has CPU/WGSL/WebGPU backends; native SIMD absent |
  | P9 | logit-level parity harness | `kernel_math_parity_test`, `ollama_kotoba_parity_test`, `inference`'s own maturity ledger |
  | P10 | tok/s benchmark against llama.cpp on the same box | murakumo's fleet benches measure the llama.cpp head, not a Kotoba one |

  P3–P5 are where vLLM and SGLang actually live, and **their decision half now
  exists in Kotoba** — see the amendment below. What is still absent there is
  the bookkeeping, which is P0 again. P1–P2 are largely WRITTEN and blocked on P0. P7–P9 exist in `.cljc`
  on the host side and were proven end to end once already:
  `cloud-murakumo-studio` generated real text from a real GGUF through
  `kotodama.inference.host.jvm` (\"The largest planet in our solar system is\"
  → \" Jupiter. Jupiter is a gas\", first token identical to Ollama).

  ## The decision

  1. **P0 is the only thing worth starting.** Two candidate shapes, both
     measured as reachable: (i) admit the aiueos bounded memory operations on
     a general native target, or (ii) admit `:vector-f64`/bytes at the export
     boundary with a copy ABI — `native-export-copy-result-type?` already
     admits vector RESULTS for exports, so half of (ii) exists.
  2. **Nothing below P0 gets built first.** A kernel that cannot be handed
     data is not a step toward an engine, and the workspace's own migration
     rule says a component the compiler cannot express is `:blocked`, not
     reduced to a smaller predicate to make a gate green.
  3. **The provider tells the truth in the meantime.** `cloud-murakumo-cli`
     refuses to enrol as capacity it does not have and names the missing
     primitive. It does not fall back to vLLM, Ollama, MLX or llama.cpp; those
     were removed on the owner's instruction, and the ADR for that decision
     records what it cost.
  4. **Parity before performance.** `ggml_kdot.clj` is already a bit-exact
     port of ggml's Q4_K/Q6_K×Q8_K reduction; any Kotoba matvec is checked
     against it before anyone measures tok/s. A fast kernel that disagrees
     with the oracle is not progress.

  ## Amendment 2026-09-09: P3–P5's decision half landed

  `kotoba-lang/inference` `e68ec8d` adds `kotoba/scheduler_core.kotoba`: 17
  exported decisions covering paged KV (`kv-blocks-needed`,
  `kv-blocks-to-append`, `kv-can-allocate` with vLLM's watermark,
  `kv-slot-index`, `kv-append-offset`, `kv-free-after-alloc`), continuous
  batching and chunked prefill (`batch-prefill-chunk`, `batch-budget-after`,
  `batch-admit`, `batch-decode-slots`, `preempt-rank`), and prefix caching and
  speculation (`prefix-cached-blocks`, `prefix-tokens-to-compute`,
  `cache-hit-bp`, `spec-accept-count`, `spec-emitted-tokens`,
  `spec-worth-it`). `amu test` runs 17 `test-*` exports across
  `[:jvm-kir :js :wasm]` — 51/51 — and it compiles to `aarch64-kotoba-v1`.

  **This is the half that is policy, and it is the half that was worth moving
  first.** vLLM's and SGLang's kernels are cuBLAS and FlashAttention; what
  those systems own is which sequence runs, how many of its tokens run this
  step, and which blocks it may hold. That is arithmetic over small integers,
  and it fits the subset every Kotoba backend already admits.

  The signatures are scalars because P0 has not moved: a native export cannot
  take a handle, and `:document` crosses the boundary but cannot be READ on
  native (only `document-edn-read`/`-print` are admitted; a `document-get`
  program is refused). So the host keeps the block table, the sequence list
  and the radix trie, and asks the core the questions those structures exist
  to answer — the same split `kernel_math_core.kotoba` makes for bytes.

  So P3–P5 are no longer `nothing`, and they are not `done` either. What
  landed is every decision; what is still behind P0 is every structure those
  decisions are made about. The ordering in this ADR is unchanged: P0 remains
  the only blocking, buildable thing.

  Two tools were repaired to land it, both broken beforehand on `main`:
  `inference`'s `nbb.edn` did not name `kotoba-lang/text`, so its KIR parity
  gate died before running; and that gate's own `--write` was unreachable
  because the guard tested only the LAST argv, so the half CI runs hid the
  half a person runs.

  ## Amendment 2026-09-09 (second): P0 and P1 measured properly, and P1 was understated

  Reading the backends rather than the docstrings changed two of the rows
  above. Both corrections make this ADR's own earlier text wrong, and the
  earlier text is the kind that sends someone to build what exists.

  ### P1 is emitted on x86-64 and absent on aarch64

  The fused dequantize-and-dot family is not \"mostly written and blocked\". It
  is a set of Kotoba OPERATIONS with machine arms:

  | operation | block bytes / elements | x86-64 | aarch64 |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-q8-0` | 34 / 32 | emitted | — |
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-q4-k` | 144 / 256 | emitted | — |
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-q6-k` | 210 / 256 | emitted | — |
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-iq4-xs` | 136 / 256 | declared, refused | — |
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-iq2-s` | 82 / 256 | declared, refused | — |
  | `kernel-dequant-dot-iq3-xxs` | 98 / 256 | declared, refused | — |
  | `kernel-dot-f32` | — | emitted | — |

  The strides are ggml's. The IQ rows carry their own reason
  (`:emitted? false`, a code in those formats is an index into a 256/512/1024
  entry table that has to reach the machine as read-only data first), which is
  a refusal with a route rather than an absence.

  **aarch64 has none of them**, and the fleet is ten Mac minis. The one
  machine that can run these kernels today is the x86-64 AIUEOS device — which
  is exactly the node class `local-murakumo.device-p256` was built around.
  That is not a coincidence to note in passing: it means the native quantized
  path already runs somewhere, and porting it to the fleet's own ISA is a
  named, bounded piece of work rather than a research question.

  ### P0's shape is a granted region, not a lifted gate

  `[:slice T]` for `T ∈ {:u8 :u16 :u32 :u64}` already exists (kotoba-sema
  ADR 0022): a source type erased into `base` and `len` machine words before
  HIR, with `slice-get`/`slice-set!`/`slice-sub`/`slice-length`, bounds
  checks, and a 2^40 element ceiling. `[:slice :f32]` is declared and refused
  because no `slice-load-f32` exists on either ISA — the u32 load plus
  `f32-from-bits` reaches the same value.

  Measured on aarch64, in order, each rejection naming the next gate:

      dot over two slices, 5 params      -> \"function parameters exceed
                                            ABI-supported arity after slice
                                            erasure: 7 machine words for 5\"
      restructured to 4 machine words    -> \"a slice parameter cannot cross an
                                            export boundary\"
      base+length in, slices built inside-> \"bounded kernel memory operation
                                            requires the aiueos kernel target\"

  So the memory family is reachable from ordinary Kotoba, and the last gate is
  the target. **It should not simply be opened.** On aiueos the program IS the
  kernel and a base address is memory it owns; on a general target a base
  handed in from outside is authority over the whole address space, and the
  bounds check validates the index against the declared length, never the base
  against anything. Opening it would put ambient authority into a language
  whose capability kits all declare `:ambient-access false`.

  P0 is therefore: **a region capability**. The host grants a bounded region
  the way `dataspace-v1` grants a dataspace — lexical/argument possession,
  no ambient access — and slice loads are admitted on general targets only
  through a granted region. The `:request`/`:result`/`:limits` shape those
  kits already use is the template, and the KEXE export copy ABI (which
  already carries vector RESULTS out of an export) is the marshalling half.

  ### What this does to the ordering

  Unchanged in shape, sharper in content:

  1. **P0 region capability** — the one gate, and now with a known design.
  2. **P1 on aarch64** — port the three emitted dequant arms to the fleet's
     ISA. The oracle for them already exists (`ggml_kdot.clj`, bit-exact
     against ggml's Q4_K/Q6_K x Q8_K reduction), so this is portable work with
     a pass/fail signal, not exploration.
  3. P3–P5 decisions are landed (previous amendment); their structures still
     wait on P0.

  ## What is honestly not claimed

  This ADR does not claim a path to llama.cpp parity on a timeline, and does
  not claim that P0 makes one likely. It claims that P0 is the first thing
  that is both blocking and buildable, that P1–P2 are mostly written behind
  it, and that P3–P5 are absent and are the largest single gap between this
  stack and the serving systems named in the instruction."}]
